      "url": "https://www.last.fm/music/Primal+Scream/Evil+Heat",
      "wiki_content": "Evil Heat is the seventh studio album by Scottish rock band Primal Scream. It was first released on 5 August 2002 in the United Kingdom by Columbia Records and on 26 November 2002 in the United States by Epic Records. It peaked at number 9 on the UK Albums Chart. Musically, its style forms a link between two of the band's previous albums: the aggressive protest of XTRMNTR (2000), and the acid house psychedelia of Screamadelica (1991) <a href=\"https://www.last.fm/music/Primal+Scream/Evil+Heat\">Read more on Last.fm</a>. User-contributed text is available under the Creative Commons By-SA License; additional terms may apply.",
      "wiki_summary": "Evil Heat is the seventh studio album by Scottish rock band Primal Scream. It was first released on 5 August 2002 in the United Kingdom by Columbia Records and on 26 November 2002 in the United States by Epic Records. It peaked at number 9 on the UK Albums Chart. Musically, its style forms a link between two of the band's previous albums: the aggressive protest of XTRMNTR (2000), and the acid house psychedelia of Screamadelica (1991) <a href=\"https://www.last.fm/music/Primal+Scream/Evil+Heat\">Read more on Last.fm</a>."
    },
    "perplexity": {
      "description": "Evil Heat is Primal Scream’s 2002 refinement of the abrasive electronic-rock vision established by XTRMNTR. Built from pounding industrial rhythms, distorted guitars, krautrock repetition, and synthetic textures, it is more streamlined and melodic than its predecessor while retaining the band’s confrontational edge. Tracks such as “Miss Lucifer,” “Rise,” and “Autobahn 66” balance dance-floor propulsion with a futuristic, hard-edged rock sound, while collaborations with Kate Moss and Robert Plant broaden the album’s palette. The result is a tense, mechanized record that helped define Primal Scream’s late-1990s-to-early-2000s experimental period, though contemporary reception was divided over its uneven focus and occasional industrial-rock conventionality.\n\nThe 2026 Bunker Trilogy reissue presents Evil Heat as the concluding chapter of the expanded series encompassing Vanishing Point, XTRMNTR, and Evil Heat. Newly remastered under the supervision of Bobby Gillespie and Andrew Innes, it adds period B-sides, rarities, previously unheard material, and new liner notes, offering a broader view of the sessions and the band’s “bunker” sound. In that context, the reissue reinforces Evil Heat’s significance as both a fierce electronic-rock statement and a key document of Primal Scream’s most adventurous era.",
